{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And the anger of HaShem burned greatly.</b> The reason for <b>greatly</b>: because for anyone who commits a transgression, given the overpowering force of the temptation and the pleasantness of the forbidden thing, there is grounds to judge him with some aspect of coercion; but those who seek to return to it — they are like ones who incite the evil inclination against themselves, and there is no greater fence against evil than this." "<b>And it was evil in the eyes of Moses.</b> For a man is particular about the work of his hands, and learn this from what is said concerning Samuel (Ta'anit 5a) — that they shortened his days so that he would not see the evil that would befall Saul; and likewise, the improper deed of Israel was evil upon Moses. Further, it intends along this line that what was evil in the eyes of Moses was that HaShem's anger burned so very greatly — as their words, may their memory be for blessing (Sifrei Part I §95), state: HaShem showed Moses the punishment He was destined to bring, and Moses said before Him: ״Master of the Universe, is it fitting that You give them meat and kill them?״ and so forth — thus far."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:07:31.310653765Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}